# Every setting a machine's .env may hold, with what it does. Do not copy this file:
#
#     make init                         # a developer machine
#     make init ENV=stage STUDIO_HOST=… ID_HOST=… SITE_DOMAIN=… [EMAIL=…]
#     make init ENV=prod  STUDIO_HOST=… ID_HOST=… [SITE_DOMAIN=…] [EMAIL=…]
#
# writes .env with ENV, the hosts and fresh secrets -- and nothing else. Copy a line from here into
# .env when you want to change something; everything not in .env comes from the environment's preset
# (env/dev.env, env/stage.env or env/prod.env) or from the defaults in docker-compose.yml (cms's own
# services) and docker-compose.standalone.yml (its copies of the shared pacod-* components). Every
# container joins the shared Docker network pacod-net (README, "Shared network").
#
# Three files, the later winning: env/<ENV>.env (tracked, no secrets), .env (this machine,
# git-ignored), .env-local (optional, git-ignored). A command-line assignment beats all three:
# `make up HTTP_PORT=8100`. `HTTP_PORT=8100 make up` does NOT work: an included file beats the
# environment. Write values bare -- no quotes, no `#`, no `$`: make and compose both read these
# files, and only a bare value means the same to both.

# ---- the environment ----------------------------------------------------------
# dev, stage or prod: which preset applies (docs/RUNNING.md). Without it: dev.
ENV=dev

# ---- addresses (stage and prod) -----------------------------------------------
# The studio's host and pacod-id's. Every other host is a website's. The websites must be on a
# different registrable domain from both -- the studio refuses to start otherwise -- because a
# website runs its owner's script, which could set cookies for a sibling host.
# STUDIO_HOST=studio.example.com
# ID_HOST=id.example.com
# Every new website answers at <id>.<PACOD_SITE_DOMAIN> first. Blank: its creator names an address.
# PACOD_SITE_DOMAIN=sites.example.net
# true lets PACOD_SITE_DOMAIN share the studio's registrable domain, which the studio otherwise refuses:
# a website's script can then set cookies the studio and pacod-id receive. Trusted websites only.
# PACOD_SITE_DOMAIN_SHARED=false
# Stage: where the two demo websites answer. Created once, on the first start of an empty platform.
# DEMO_HOSTS=demo.sites.example.net
# DEMO_MINIMAL_HOSTS=minimal.sites.example.net
# The addresses browsers use: derived from the hosts and the preset's scheme and port (https:// on
# stage and prod, http://<host>:8000 on dev). Set only to override. pacod-id registers them exactly.
# STUDIO_ORIGIN=https://studio.example.com
# ID_ISSUER=https://id.example.com
# How the studio spells a website's address (previews, "view site"). Unset: the profile decides --
# http and HTTP_PORT on dev, https and port 443 on stage and prod.
# SITE_PUBLIC_SCHEME=https
# SITE_PUBLIC_PORT=443
# Caddy's site addresses for the studio and the websites (platform/caddy/cms.caddy): derived from
# STUDIO_HOST and the preset's scheme. Set only to override; spaces after the commas are fine here.
# CMS_STUDIO_SITE=https://studio.example.com
# CMS_PUBLIC_SITE=https://

# ---- the first account --------------------------------------------------------
# Created once by pacod-id, a platform admin of the studio; its password is ID_ADMIN_PASSWORD below.
# Production: a mailbox someone reads (password resets go there).
ID_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@pacod-cms.test

# ---- mail (prod: required) ----------------------------------------------------
# pacod-id's mail: confirmations, invitations, password resets. Unset: pacod-mailpit catches it (dev
# and stage); production refuses to start without a relay. TLS: starttls (the default for a relay),
# tls or none.
# PID_SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# PID_SMTP_PORT=587
# PID_SMTP_TLS=starttls
# PID_SMTP_USERNAME=id@example.com
# PID_SMTP_PASSWORD=
# The sender. Production: an address on a domain the relay may send for (not .test).
# ID_MAIL_FROM=id@example.com

# ---- AI (pacod-gw) ------------------------------------------------------------
# dev and stage run the mock gateway (pacod-gw, http://pacod-gw:8080, the default address) with a
# key make init writes. Production names a real gateway, or AI stays off ("not set up"). The model:
# a pool or template of the gateway (@fast, the mock gateway's pool) or a model id.
# PACOD_GW_URL=https://gw.example.com
# PACOD_GW_API_KEY=
# PACOD_AI_MODEL=@fast

# ---- certificates (stage and prod) --------------------------------------------
# Caddy obtains every certificate itself (Let's Encrypt; a website's on demand, once the site
# confirms the host). Its notices go to this address:
# CADDY_GLOBAL_OPTIONS=email ops@example.com
# A pair of your own, optional: Caddy serves it for every name it covers, and obtains certificates
# for the rest. Host paths; the .crt a FULL CHAIN, leaf first. After a renewal: make caddy-reload.
# SSL_CERT_FILE=/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem
# SSL_KEY_FILE=/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem

# ---- secrets (make init writes them) ------------------------------------------
# Keep .env private and backed up. Once data exists they cannot be replaced: MongoDB took its two
# passwords on its first start, pacod-id's database and role were created with PID_PG_* and
# PID_DB_PASSWORD, and what pacod-id stored is sealed under PID_KEK. make init refuses to write
# them anew while that data exists.
MONGO_ROOT_USER=pacod-root
# MONGO_ROOT_PASSWORD=
MONGO_USER=pacod-cms
# MONGO_PASSWORD=
# PID_PG_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=
# PID_DB_PASSWORD=
# PID_KEK=
# PID_TOKEN_PEPPER=
# CMS_ID_STUDIO_SECRET=
# CMS_ID_SERVICE_SECRET=
# ID_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# The key the studio signs draft previews with and the site checks; the same value for both, at
# least 32 characters. Blank: no previews.
# PACOD_PREVIEW_SECRET=
# Salts the opaque reader key behind "readers now"; no address or User-Agent is stored. Blank: a
# new salt at every restart, which double-counts current readers once.
# METRICS_VISITOR_SALT=
# The studio's provisioner in pacod-id, which makes an organisation's creator its admin: written by
# make up (make id-provisioner). Without it only platform admins create organisations.
# PACOD_ID_PROVISIONER_ACCOUNT=
# PACOD_ID_PROVISIONER_TOKEN=

# ---- optional settings (every one has a default) --------------------------------
# The studio's idle session (Spring Boot duration). After it, pacod-id's own session signs the
# person straight back in.
# MGMT_SESSION_TIMEOUT=8h
#
# The portal: self-service (anyone signed in may create an organisation) or platform-admins (a
# ceiling the installation screen cannot lift); the limits and the AI default are starting values
# the installation screen owns once saved.
# PACOD_ORG_CREATION=self-service
# PACOD_MAX_ORGS_PER_USER=3
# PACOD_MAX_SITES_PER_ORG=5
# PACOD_AI_FOR_NEW_ORGS=false
#
# What an article image must be: exactly WIDTHxHEIGHT and at most MAX_BYTES (131072 or 2MB). The two
# checks switch independently; "off" is not unlimited: Caddy and the servlet container refuse above
# 4 MiB whatever these say, and the screens state that ceiling. Read at startup: recreate the studio.
# IMAGE_REQUIRED_WIDTH=600
# IMAGE_REQUIRED_HEIGHT=400
# IMAGE_MAX_BYTES=131072
# IMAGE_DIMENSION_CHECK=true
# IMAGE_FILE_SIZE_CHECK=true
#
# Crawlers (/mgmt/crawlers): TRACKING=false stops the log, crawlers stay out of the reader numbers
# either way; VERIFY=false skips the reverse/forward DNS check that tells a real Googlebot from a
# forged one (it needs outbound DNS from the site container).
# CRAWLER_TRACKING=true
# CRAWLER_VERIFY=true
#
# Browser origins allowed to call the applications, as patterns (`[*]` for any port; a bare `*` is
# refused). Unset: localhost on any port -- set it in production.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:[*],https://www.example.com
#
# PDF export renders through the stack's Gotenberg; blank switches it off. Background jobs: how many
# run at once, and how many of one organisation's.
# PACOD_PDF_GOTENBERG_URL=http://cms-gotenberg:3000
# PACOD_JOBS_WORKERS=2
# PACOD_JOBS_PER_ORGANISATION=1
#
# The images built elsewhere: pacod-id (make images builds it from PACOD_ID_SRC when missing) and the
# mock gateway (from PACOD_GW_SRC, dev and stage).
# PACOD_ID_IMAGE=pacod-id:dev
# PACOD_ID_SRC=../pacod-id
# PACOD_GW_SRC=../pacod-gw
#
# Logging (pacod-demo/docs/LOGGING.md). cms-site and cms-studio: the level -- debug, info, warn
# (or warning), error, any case; anything else is info -- and stdout's format, json or text (the
# files under LOG_DIR_HOST, <service>.log, are JSON either way). env/dev.env sets text. Caddy's
# access log: json or console. Recreate the containers after a change (make up).
# APP_LOG_LEVEL=info
# APP_LOG_FORMAT=json
# CMS_CADDY_LOG_FORMAT=json
#
# A preset's value, changed for this machine only -- ports, host paths, profiles:
# HTTP_PORT=8100
# MONGO_DATA_DIR=/srv/pacod/mongo
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=mailpit
#
# TLS inside the JVM, off: Caddy terminates TLS. See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md before turning it on.
# SSL_ENABLED=false
